QEMU's user mode answers a guest's getrandom from the host, which
-seed does not reach: two runs of one program draw different entropy,
and every instruction computing on it differs between them. The host
still answers the call, so its flags are checked and its length and
errors chosen as the kernel would; the bytes it returned are then
drawn from the guest generator, which -seed makes deterministic and
which, unseeded, draws from the host.

--- a/linux-user/syscall.c
+++ b/linux-user/syscall.c
@@ -10899,6 +10899,14 @@
             return -TARGET_EFAULT;
         }
         ret = get_errno(getrandom(p, arg2, arg3));
+        if (ret > 0) {
+            /*
+             * The host checked the flags and chose the length; the
+             * bytes come from the guest generator, which -seed makes
+             * deterministic.
+             */
+            qemu_guest_getrandom_nofail(p, ret);
+        }
         unlock_user(p, arg1, ret);
         return ret;
 #endif
